{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-354137",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Consider the rightmost petal, oriented along the $x$-axis. First, we find the values of $\\theta $ for which the two curves intersect:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta ) & =3 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $5\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ]:$\n\n$$\n5\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{10},\\frac{\\pi }{10}\n$$\n\nTo determine which is the inner function and which is the outer, let's draw an arrow from the origin that passes through the shaded region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14248834/assets/5eee50d0dbcf5c7cb6969416c81eeb8a54eb7ef4df1262e69324718108fc33fa.png)\n\nThe arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $r=3,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $r=3+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta ).$\n\nTo find the area, we use the formula\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$\\,$ ${\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{10},\\,$ and $\\,{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{10}.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}((3+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta ))^{2}-(3)^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}(9+12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta )+4{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )-9)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}(12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta )+4{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}(12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta )+2(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10\\theta )))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[\\frac{12}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta )+2\\theta +\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10\\theta )]}_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[(\\frac{12}{5}+\\frac{12}{5})+(\\frac{\\pi }{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{5})+0] \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total shaded area is\n\n$$\nA=5{A}_{1}=5(\\frac{12}{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{5})=12+\\pi .\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
